1:["$","main",null,{"className":"site article-page","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"article-frame","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"desktop-brand","href":"/","children":["$","span",null,{"className":"letter-cascade is-in","aria-label":"Thinkinghaus","children":[["$","b","T-0",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":0},"children":"T"}],["$","b","h-1",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":1},"children":"h"}],["$","b","i-2",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":2},"children":"i"}],["$","b","n-3",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":3},"children":"n"}],["$","b","k-4",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":4},"children":"k"}],["$","b","i-5",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":5},"children":"i"}],["$","b","n-6",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":6},"children":"n"}],["$","b","g-7",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":7},"children":"g"}],["$","b","h-8",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":8},"children":"h"}],["$","b","a-9",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":9},"children":"a"}],["$","b","u-10",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":10},"children":"u"}],["$","b","s-11",{"aria-hidden":"true","style":{"--letter-cascade-index":11},"children":"s"}]]}]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"article-column","data-content-slug":"personal-folklore","data-content-title":"Personal folklore","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"article-header","children":[["$","h1",null,{"children":"Personal folklore"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"July 14, 2026"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"3 minute read"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["By ",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://maxpfennig.haus/","rel":"author","children":"Max Pfennighaus"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"author-edit-action","hidden":true,"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://thinkinghaus-studio.maxpfennighaus.workers.dev/","children":"Edit"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"article-body","children":[[["$","p","0-Repeated self-observation does something strange over time. At first I’m recording events: this happened, I felt this, I made this mistake. The journal is evidence.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["Repeated self-observation does something strange over time. At first I’m recording events: this happened, I felt this, I made this mistake. The journal is evidence."]}],["$","p","1-But some things return—the same fear in different clothes, the same bad bargain becoming tempting again, a particular kind of excitement I’ve learned to trust, the moment when I start making a simple thing complicated because complication feels safer than finishing it. Eventually the particulars begin to fall away.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["But some things return—the same fear in different clothes, the same bad bargain becoming tempting again, a particular kind of excitement I’ve learned to trust, the moment when I start making a simple thing complicated because complication feels safer than finishing it. Eventually the particulars begin to fall away."]}],["$","p","2-Dru Riley’s “[100 rules](https://druriley.com/100-2/)” feel like the residue of this process. He says each rule points back to a personal story. Somewhere behind Dru’s *don’t self-reject* or *make it work, THEN make it pretty* is an incident I’ll never know. The incident has disappeared, but its shape remains. That feels a lot like folklore: experience getting [worn smooth through repetition](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Peter_Enthroned_in_Saint_Peters_Basilica.jpg) until what survives is the part somebody decided was worth carrying forward.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["Dru Riley’s “",["$","a","1",{"href":"https://druriley.com/100-2/","rel":"noreferrer","children":["100 rules"]}],"” feel like the residue of this process. He says each rule points back to a personal story. Somewhere behind Dru’s ",["$","em","3",{"children":"$Ld"}]," or ","$Le"," is an incident I’ll never know. The incident has disappeared, but its shape remains. That feels a lot like folklore: experience getting ","$Lf"," until what survives is the part somebody decided was worth carrying forward."]}],"$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13"],null]}],"$L14","$L15"]}]]}]}]
d:["don’t self-reject"]
e:["$","em","5",{"children":["make it work, THEN make it pretty"]}]
f:["$","a","7",{"href":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Peter_Enthroned_in_Saint_Peters_Basilica.jpg","rel":"noreferrer","children":["worn smooth through repetition"]}]
10:["$","p","3-A family has three [disastrous camping trips](https://www.nps.gov/yell/blogs/truman-c-everts-yellowstone-s-first-unlucky-adventurer.htm) and ten years later everyone says, “We’re not camping people.” Nobody remembers the rainstorm, the broken tent, or who was miserable first. The incidents blur together and the verdict survives. I think I’m doing this with myself all the time. One episode comes to stand for ten others. I forget where a conviction came from but keep carrying it around. Past me leaves behind warnings, superstitions, shortcuts, little scraps of alleged wisdom for whoever I become next.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["A family has three ",["$","a","1",{"href":"https://www.nps.gov/yell/blogs/truman-c-everts-yellowstone-s-first-unlucky-adventurer.htm","rel":"noreferrer","children":["disastrous camping trips"]}]," and ten years later everyone says, “We’re not camping people.” Nobody remembers the rainstorm, the broken tent, or who was miserable first. The incidents blur together and the verdict survives. I think I’m doing this with myself all the time. One episode comes to stand for ten others. I forget where a conviction came from but keep carrying it around. Past me leaves behind warnings, superstitions, shortcuts, little scraps of alleged wisdom for whoever I become next."]}]
11:["$","p","4-Some of them are useful and some are probably complete nonsense, which is what makes the whole thing interesting. Folklore isn’t truth. “I always do this” is folklore. “I’m not the kind of person who…” is folklore too. A story can begin as a pretty accurate description and quietly harden into identity. Three bad camping trips become a family trait.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["Some of them are useful and some are probably complete nonsense, which is what makes the whole thing interesting. Folklore isn’t truth. “I always do this” is folklore. “I’m not the kind of person who…” is folklore too. A story can begin as a pretty accurate description and quietly harden into identity. Three bad camping trips become a family trait."]}]
12:["$","p","5-This makes a journal seem less like a record of a stable self and more like a place where a mythology is being manufactured in real time. I’m remembering, simplifying, exaggerating, finding patterns that are probably real and occasionally drawing a constellation between completely unrelated stars. Years later, I inherit the whole mess from the people I used to be: useful warnings alongside old misunderstandings, good instincts alongside conclusions that may have expired without telling me.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["This makes a journal seem less like a record of a stable self and more like a place where a mythology is being manufactured in real time. I’m remembering, simplifying, exaggerating, finding patterns that are probably real and occasionally drawing a constellation between completely unrelated stars. Years later, I inherit the whole mess from the people I used to be: useful warnings alongside old misunderstandings, good instincts alongside conclusions that may have expired without telling me."]}]
13:["$","p","6-Riley’s list doesn’t feel like a finished philosophy so much as personal folklore that has been [compressed down to a portable size](https://www.oupjapan.co.jp/en/products/detail/2632?language=en). The interesting question isn’t whether I can eventually produce my own hundred rules. It’s which stories I keep telling myself, where they came from, and whether they’re still true.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["Riley’s list doesn’t feel like a finished philosophy so much as personal folklore that has been ",["$","a","1",{"href":"https://www.oupjapan.co.jp/en/products/detail/2632?language=en","rel":"noreferrer","children":["compressed down to a portable size"]}],". The interesting question isn’t whether I can eventually produce my own hundred rules. It’s which stories I keep telling myself, where they came from, and whether they’re still true."]}]
